  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/49/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_50.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The splash page tells us we're about to meet the \"most feared Oriental of all time!\"&nbsp; The Mandarin has been lurking in the \"vastness of Red China,\" and Iron Man is about to face him!\n\nThe Mandarin is feared in the Orient, and four high-ranking military officers of the Red government seek an audience with him.&nbsp; We get to see his castle as he agrees to hear what they have to say.&nbsp; The officials fear him, but their government has ordered them to come and the Mandarin to share the secrets of his fearsome power rings!&nbsp; But Mandarin will have none of that -- he will share his secrets with <i>no</i> one!&nbsp; He grants them 60 seconds to flee for their lives!\n\nBack in the States, the military has contacted Iron Man to learn more about this \"Mandarin\" character that the red government is so fearful of.&nbsp; For their own security, any threat that powerful is something they ought to learn more about.&nbsp; Iron Man prepares to head for Red China, but he first has to excuse himself from an employee dinner as Tony Stark, which gives the narrative a moment to check in on Pepper and Happy.\n\nHours later, Iron Man is shown hopping off a jet flying near Red China, and he bails out, making sure that any who see him think that no chute opened and that he simply plummeted to his death.&nbsp; Now on the ground, Iron Man walks for hours before finally coming to \"the castle of the <i>Mandarin</i>!\"&nbsp; He encounters the private guards of the Mandarin and makes short work of them, but Mandarin sees him approaching.&nbsp; A simple death trap is easily avoided, and the Mandarin welcomes Iron Man into his presence, telling him he'll never leave alive.\n\nAs Iron Man uses his gadgets to try and topple the Mandarin, the latter uses one ring after another to ward off the attacks, and Iron Man realizes each one is a different weapon, and the \"paralyzer ray\" soon has Iron Man frozen in his tracks!&nbsp; But Iron Man recovers faster than expected, and the Mandarin then reveals that he's also the \"greatest karate master the world has ever known.\"&nbsp; As he approaches to finish off Iron Man with his bare hands, the armor on his left arm opens to reveal Iron Man's \"built-in slide rule calculator.\"\n\nThe astonished Mandarin hesitates long enough to allow Iron Man to calculate the exact angle needed to ward off the ensuing karate blows and ensure that it's the <i>Mandarin's</i> arm that will be hurt (instead of his armor).&nbsp; With him crumpled in pain, Iron Man heads out to catch his \"pick-up plane.\"\n\nIron Man is able to get back just in time for Tony to attend part of the employee dinner after all, and Pepper is annoyed now because she decided to attend it with Happy and now thinks the boss won't look her way if he thinks the two are a couple!\n\nBut the Mandarin sits stewing in his castle, and we're left \"wondering when and where the powerful Oriental menace will <i>strike</i> again!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "An intriguing villain debut that stops short of inspiring anticipation.\nBut Iron Man's \"advanced\" technology cements the issue forever in our minds!",
  "subtitle": "Ten Rings to... uh... \"Rule\" Them All",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | FormidableMandarin | width=15%] Mandarin is clearly a formidable foe, feared even by his own government, but it seems like Red China realizes he's a threat to <i>them</i> as well, and you have to wonder why the Chinese authorities would not deal with this nearby menace themselves, rather than leaving him free to plot who-knows-what.\n[BR]\n[BR] The issue wastes little time showing how dangerous Mandarin is supposed to be.&nbsp; He casually threatens high-ranking government officials and treats them as though they are beneath his notice.&nbsp; The story is so eager to establish his menace, in fact, that Mandarin barely has to do anything before everyone around him is already terrified.\n\nWhat's interesting is that the story seems determined to establish Mandarin as an enemy of <b><i>everyone</i></b>.&nbsp; He isn't presented as an agent of Red China, but rather as an [SPAN: severity=error; text=unpredictable force that even the Chinese government fears.]&nbsp; That immediately sets him apart from the more straightforward Communist villains that have appeared elsewhere in Marvel's pages.\n\nFormidable as he is, it came as no surprise to discover that the Mandarin lives in... <i>where else?...</i> a [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=castle] (which pushes the count up to three, just this month <i>alone</i> -- see [LINK:1963:JIM_100_thor0|JIM #100|2027-03-04] and [LINK:1963:TTA_52_giant0|TTA #52|2027-03-07]), but at least in this case, \"the most feared Oriental of all time\" (quoted from the splash page) seems the very type to actually <i>live</i> in one, so it doesn't play as oddly as it did with the other two before it.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>This </i>one even has a drawbridge!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | Dishrag | width=15%] Pepper and Happy, as we so often find, appear shoe-horned into the story simply because Stan believes readers now <i>expect</i> them to appear, even though the story really only required that readers focus on Iron Man.\n[BR]\n[BR] Page 4 in particular (the one where Tony excuses himself from the employees' dinner) is particularly gratuitous and seems there only so that we don't forget they are supporting characters, but it very much has the flavor of \"punching in\" for the month.\n\n[ASSET: right | HappyAndPepperDate | width=12%] The Pepper and Happy moments are... <i>uh</i>... \"peppered\" throughout the issue, which in this case feels unnatural, and one wonders if perhaps an extra page or two of Iron Man versus the Mandarin might have been a better choice from a storytelling perspective.&nbsp; That being said, it was still good to see the two finally forced to attend the dinner together (though Pepper is none too happy about it!).\n\n[ASSET: left | SlantyEyes | width=15%] The Chinese are consistently depicted with slanty eyes that rarely seem to open, something modern readers might find offensive, and the term \"Oriental\" is liberally sprinkled throughout the narrative.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is the Silver Age, after all, and it was not at all uncommon to find this type of portrayal in what could easily have been classified as a \"Vintage Moment.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I realize this is Scenario Bending insight, but in this case, I felt the need to do the callout here, as the <i>true</i> Vintage Moment of the issue occurs later.)]\n\n[ASSET: right | PowerMadDespot | width=12%] The fact that Mandarin seems able to speak fluent English is something that is never even given lip service.&nbsp; <i>Of course he can</i> -- for this magazine, that's almost a <i>given</i>.&nbsp; So too is the plot acceleration on page 7, where after only three panels of face-to-face conversation, Iron Man already decides Mandarin is a \"power-mad despot\" that he must take down or \"die trying!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Again, it's the Silver Age, and there <i>are</i> only six pages left after all!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | BattleScene | width=40%] [ASSET: right | KarateMaster | width=15%] The battle scenes ensue, and Iron Man finally realizes what is already patently obvious to readers:&nbsp; each of the power rings comes with its own weapon.\n[BR]\n[BR] And if that weren't bad enough, even <i>without</i> the rings, Mandarin is (of course), a \"karate master\" to boot.\n[BR] Is there no way to stop this... <i>fiend</i>?\n\nIn fairness, giving each ring a different power creates immediate uncertainty for both Iron Man and the reader.&nbsp; Every time Mandarin raises a hand, neither knows exactly what will happen next, which gives the battle a level of unpredictability which may have been lacking in some of Iron Man's previous encounters.\n\nApparently Stan wanted readers to understand that defeating the rings alone would not be enough.&nbsp; Mandarin needed to remain dangerous even after being separated from his primary weapons.&nbsp; So in this sense, even if one objects to the premise of the Swiss Army Knife power rings, the Mandarin still represents a formidable opponent even <i>without</i> them.\n\nThe <i>tour-de-force</i> is the unveiling of the most awesome weapon of all!&nbsp; Lo and behold, Iron Man's mighty \"built-in-slide-rule calculater [sic].\"&nbsp; <i>Yeah baby</i>!&nbsp; Every armor should be equipped with such a gadget!&nbsp; After all, one never knows when advanced <i>mathematics</i> might prove more useful than rockets.&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!&nbsp; </i>Even Mandarin is \"so amused by Iron Man's apparent mood of madness\" that he has to pause.\n\n[ASSET: center | BuiltInSlideRule | width=67%]\n(And can we really blame him for being astonished at a \"slide rule calculator\" in this advanced suit of armor?)\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I say this all very tongue-in-cheek of course], but fun fact:&nbsp; this scene was <i>so unintentionally hilarious</i> that I awarded an additional half-point for it <i>alone</i>.&nbsp; Pardon me for a moment while I temporarily step <i>out</i> of [PAGE: scenario] mode:\n\n[INDENT: both | 3rem] Obviously this was published in the 1960s, and calculators debuted about ten years after this issue was published, but seeing this image -- this \"advanced\" technology that gave Iron Man's armor its own \"built-in-slide-rule\" -- was what finally led me to create the Vintage Moment Award in the first place (and coin the term \"VMA\" for what is now commonly found in so many of my monthly summaries).\n[BR]\n[BR] Up to this point, I had simply laughed them off and dismissed the notion entirely, [SPAN: severity=success; text=but this was a moment too good not to mention!]\n[BR]\n[BR] And so... the Vintage Moment Award was born!\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And I suspect you can now guess what this month's winner will <i>be</i>!)]\n\n(Stepping back <i>into</i> Scenario mode now...)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nEven if we ignore the fact that Iron Man hops a jet headed back to the United States and arrives at Idlewild Airport in time to make that evening's employee dinner (a virtual impossibility in and of itself), [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=what about Iron Man's report back to the US government officials?]\n\nFor a story that forced Iron Man to head to Red China to investigate the threat of the Mandarin, it [SPAN: severity=error; text=never even addresses the subject] on its final page, instead showing Tony finally showing up at the employee dinner (where of course Pepper is mortified to be seen with a \"big ox\" like Happy).\n\nReaders might have expected the plane to head back to Washington DC (instead of New York City) so that Iron Man could be debriefed... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=but no!]&nbsp; The story doesn't bother to broach the subject again, so readers are forced to assume that perhaps this happened while en route back to the States.&nbsp; (<i>But who knows?</i>)\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=In effect, the story remembers that Iron Man must get home, but forgets why he was sent out in the first place.]\n\nIron Man raced out of Mandarin's castle in time to catch his plane back, and if the episode teaches us anything, it's that the US <i>should</i> be worried about the Mandarin... but <i>now</i> what?\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>DISCLAIMER (Scenario-Bending):</b>\n[BR] I feel obliged to admit right up front that, while I am a <i>huge</i> fan of Iron Man, I have <i>never</i> been a fan of the Mandarin.&nbsp; The ten rings have always seemed to me to be a crutch for the author and are too tempting to use for \"what power can we pull out of the hat next?\" gimmicks.&nbsp; (Not to mention that I've never been big on stories that feature far-eastern threats, as the whole judo/karate thing is just not for me in general.)&nbsp; This is the first time I've ever bothered to sit down and read the issue where he makes his first appearance.\n\nLike Doctor Strange though, I promise to try and keep an open mind with regard to the Mandarin (and his all-too-many upcoming appearances in these pages.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThis is a hard issue to read and stay in the moment (and you can see that I've had to dance around [PAGE: scenario] multiple times in my review).\n\nThe long and short of it is that it seems Stan Lee was determined to give Iron Man a nemesis, like the Fantastic Four have Dr. Doom and Doctor Strange has Baron Mordo.&nbsp; Whether the Mandarin is the right choice for Iron Man remains to be seen (and I confess I haven't read enough of the early <i>Tales of Suspense</i> books to know, so at least that's <i>something</i>).\n\nThe point of the half-length tale seems to have been to shove the new Mandarin out in front of readers, show us in the early going that he is feared (even by his own government), and treat us to just a small <i>taste</i> of his true mettle for now.&nbsp; It may have been a little too brief, but the episode nevertheless feels more significant than some of Iron Man's other battles of late, so that argues for a better-than-reader-level experience.\n\nCramming Happy and Pepper into the plot was questionable, but I <i>do</i> still find their banter enjoyable and especially like the moments where Pepper thinks of Happy, even though she obviously prefers her own boss (what is it with these Marvel women who fall in love with their bosses?&nbsp; See \"Jane Foster\" and in some sense, even \"Janet Van Dyne,\" who continues to refer to Hank as \"boss man.\")&nbsp; For his part, Happy is <i>always</i> pleased when she even gives him the time of day!\n\nThe lack of a clear debriefing moment at the issue's end was a bit weak, but the first-ever VMA more than makes up for it and even breaks the issue wide open for its absolutely unintentional (and retrospective) hilarity.&nbsp; Even looking at it through 1960s lenses, the notion of Iron Man pausing in mid-fight to check a computation strikes one as funny, and it was so outrageous, in fact, that it made the Mandarin pause in astonishment.\n\nThat's just too good not to give extra credit for!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nSomewhere in his castle, the Mandarin is still brooding over the fact that his historic debut was upstaged by a pocket calculator that wasn't even a calculator!",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:</b>  \"Journey's End\"\n\nThe Watcher reads from his \"cosmic log\" and tells the story of Wilbur Weems, a socially-awkward individual who is ridiculed by his fellow man in the 21<sup>st</sup> century, driving him to sign up for an obvious one-way mission into space to investigate a strange radiation that could interfere with space travel.&nbsp; The volunteer pilot has no <i>chance</i> of returning.\n\nSince he has the least to lose, Weems volunteers.&nbsp; He actually gets to the \"next galaxy\" where he is transformed into a hunk by the people who set up the radiation to test for brave adventurers who would go even with no chance of returning.&nbsp; He's reached paradise, and the Watcher turns toward the reader to encourage us to boldly go about our daily tasks, knowing that we are never alone!\n\nAgain, the Watcher breaking the 4<sup>th</sup> wall allows him to leave the reader with a moral at the end, which seems to be the point:&nbsp; to use the tale as a kind of parable, all to give him a bully-pulpit from which he can relate some \"tale of suspense\" which has a lesson to be learned.\n\nHis use like this certainly sets him apart from other characters in the Marvel Universe, so it's not clear yet if this is the best use of the Watcher, but at least it's somewhat in character...."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "I noted on the top-right panel of page 6, readers get a close-up of Iron Man's fist, which ultimately becomes one of the hallmark looks for the character once George Tuska picks up the art duties -- something I call the \"disembodied fist punch!\"&nbsp; This was the first time I've seen the look, done here by Don Heck, but <i>boy</i> did it bring back a lot of fond memories of the later Tuska issues!",
  "highlight": "A new villain arrives in the form of the Mandarin, but the <i>real</i> star is Iron Man's calculator."
}